                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Diagnose: read the network`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `The ONA outputs are in front of you — a knowledge-flow map of all 540 people aggregated to squads and key individuals, centrality scores, density metrics and the flight-risk overlay — beside the org chart for contrast. The discovery to make: <strong>the org chart and the real knowledge network look nothing alike</strong>. Using a social-network lens, name the <em>connectors</em> (high betweenness), the most damaging <em>structural holes</em> between siloed squads, and the over-concentration risk: <strong>3 connectors mediate ~45% of cross-team ties</strong>, and you just watched one departure break multiple flows at once.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Plan: where to invest the network`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Design the intervention portfolio under a <strong>fixed development budget and zero new headcount</strong>. Three levers: which <em>connectors</em> to invest in (protect and de-load the central few, or cultivate new ones to cut single-point risk), which <em>silos to bridge</em> and via which mechanism, and how to deploy <em>line managers</em> as knowledge enablers. The discovery: closing the <strong>right structural hole</strong> between two squads lifts the transfer index more than pouring development into an already-central person — and tacit knowledge moves through relationships, not a wiki.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Decide: commit under the headcount cap`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `The core round. Commit finite budget and scarce managerial capacity across competing moves: <em>knowledge-capture and succession</em> for the at-risk connectors, <em>bridge-building</em> (rotations, paired work, internal mobility, communities of practice) to raise density, and <em>redistribution of brokering load</em> so the network survives the next exit. Managers' time and rotation slots are scarce — <strong>not every silo can be bridged this cycle</strong>. Protecting current connectors stabilises flow now but entrenches concentration; cultivating new bridges builds resilience but pays off slower.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Recover: stress-test the network and defend the plan`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Two shocks hit. <strong>A second top connector signals an exit</strong> — the flight-risk you were warned about materialises — and <strong>a new strategic client demands rapid cross-squad mobilization</strong>. Show your reshaped network absorbs the loss: does knowledge now flow through multiple paths, do the bridges carry load, can you mobilize across squads <em>without a single hero</em>? Then make the 3-minute case to the executive committee on the four KPIs — and make the rigid reorganization unnecessary.`,

