                    coach_default:          `Intervention logged.`,
                    coach_r1_network:       `<strong>That's the right lens.</strong> Work flows through the network of ties, not the reporting lines. Kaše's whole point is that capability lives in the relationships between people — so the connectors, the holes and the density are what you manage, not the boxes.`,
                    coach_r1_bothequal:     `<strong>Averaging the two pictures blurs the signal.</strong> The org chart tells you who reports to whom; the ONA tells you where knowledge actually moves. They diverge sharply here — and it's the divergence, not the average, that just cost you a stalled flagship.`,
                    coach_r1_orgchart:      `<strong>Managing by the org chart is exactly how Triglav got here.</strong> The reporting lines never showed that 45% of cross-team knowledge ran through three people. The boxes look fine; the network is one departure from breaking. Read the ONA, not the hierarchy.`,
                    coach_r1_concentration: `<strong>You've found the live wire.</strong> Three connectors mediating ~45% of cross-team ties is a textbook single point of failure — and you just watched one departure sever multiple flows. Centrality is an asset and a risk at once; the risk is what's burning.`,
                    coach_r1_density:       `<strong>Density is low, but “more ties everywhere, fast” isn't the diagnosis.</strong> The dangerous feature is that the few ties you have run through three people. Raising density blindly is expensive and slow; cutting the concentration is the urgent move.`,
                    coach_r1_lowpay:        `<strong>This isn't a pay problem.</strong> The connectors aren't central because they're cheap — they're central because knowledge has been allowed to route through them for years. A raise doesn't widen the network; it just makes the single point of failure more expensive.`,
                    coach_r1_all:           `<strong>You can't close fifteen holes at once.</strong> Your managers have capacity for maybe four, and half your engineers would be in transit instead of delivering. Density has a cost — the diagnosis is which <em>two or three</em> holes unlock the most cross-squad flow.`,
                    coach_r1_dense:         `<strong>Reinforcing the strong ties adds redundancy where you already have it.</strong> The leverage is in the structural holes — the squad pairs with <em>zero</em> direct ties that force everything through the connectors. Bridge the gaps, don't thicken the bridges that already hold.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You read the network instead of the org chart, named the concentration as the live risk, and targeted the two or three highest-leverage holes. That's the read that lets every later euro land where it lifts whole-network flow.`,
                    coach_r2_deload:        `<strong>De-load and surround the central few — that's the move.</strong> You stabilise the connectors you have while building bridges around them, so the next departure doesn't sever the network. You're reducing dependence on heroes instead of deepening it.`,
                    coach_r2_relational:    `<strong>De-load the connectors and move tacit knowledge through relationships — exactly right.</strong> Rotations, paired work and communities of practice are how the context in someone's head actually transfers. Concentration falls, density rises, and the network stops being one person deep.`,
                    coach_r2_deepen:        `<strong>You poured everything into your two remaining connectors.</strong> They're now even more central — and even more single-points-of-failure, with one already flight-risk. Did you reduce the network's dependence on heroes, or deepen it? Concentration just went the wrong way.`,
                    coach_r2_hire:          `<strong>You posted a req for a new principal architect.</strong> Two problems: the CEO authorized zero net new headcount, and even if you hired, an outsider arrives with <em>no ties</em> — the network hole stays open for a year while they build relationships. Kaše's point is that you fix the network with the people you have.`,
                    coach_r2_wiki:          `<strong>A knowledge-base sprint won't capture what made Maja central.</strong> The context she carried doesn't fit in a wiki — that's tacit knowledge, and it moves through relationships, not documents. What relational mechanism transfers what a page can't?`,
                    coach_r2_reorg:         `<strong>Conceding the rigid reorg this early is surrender.</strong> Your engineering leaders warn it will make the silos permanent — structure forced from above doesn't create the cross-squad ties tacit knowledge needs. The whole brief is to make the reorg unnecessary.`,
                    coach_r2_underfunded:   `<strong>Right mechanism, starved of budget.</strong> Rotations and communities of practice cost manager time and engineer hours — fund them below a quarter of the budget and the bridges never carry real load. Ties have a cost; pay for the ones you chose.`,
                    coach_r2_supervise:     `<strong>Your managers are running their squads efficiently — and entirely inward.</strong> A line manager is a node with reach into a team; deployed right, they're a bridge. Used only as supervisors, they reinforce the very silos you're trying to break.`,
                    coach_r2_lmignore:      `<strong>Leaving managers out of the network plan wastes your best bridges.</strong> Each manager has reach across a whole squad — that's exactly the position from which to broker ties to other squads. An ONA project that ignores managers is missing half the network.`,
                    coach_r3_document:      `<strong>Playbooks capture the explicit, not the tacit.</strong> Documenting the connectors' work is useful housekeeping, but it doesn't transfer the cross-squad context that made them central. Pair them with apprentices and the tacit knowledge moves through the relationship.`,
                    coach_r3_nosuccession:  `<strong>Skipping succession on people you just watched become single points of failure is a bet against your own ONA.</strong> One already left; two are flight-risk. Build the apprenticeship now, while the connectors are still here to teach.`,
                    coach_r3_flood:         `<strong>Maximum rotation across all squads at once exhausts your scarce capacity.</strong> Half your engineers in transit means delivery slips and the bridges are too thin to hold. Targeted rotations onto the highest-leverage holes beat a flood every time.`,
                    coach_r3_nomobility:    `<strong>Zero rotations means zero new cross-squad ties.</strong> Internal mobility isn't only a retention tool — it's the instrument that builds the relationships knowledge transfer needs. Freeze mobility entirely and density never moves.`,
                    coach_r3_newheroes:     `<strong>Anointing two new star connectors just rebuilds the trap with new names.</strong> Routing everything through them recreates the single-point-of-failure you set out to fix. Resilience comes from multiple redundant paths, not from a fresh set of heroes.`,
                    coach_r3_protect:       `<strong>Keeping all the flow on the proven connectors feels safe and isn't.</strong> It's fast today and fragile tomorrow — the next exit severs the same flows. Spread the load across the bridges so no one departure can break the network.`,
                    coach_r3_noretention:   `<strong>No retention spend on two flight-risk connectors is a bet on the bridges arriving in time.</strong> They won't, this quarter — the central people have offers now. Fund the de-load and the stay-conversations, or you'll lose a second connector before the bridges carry load.`,
                    coach_r3_resilient:     `<strong>That's a resilient design.</strong> Apprentices carry the tacit context, targeted rotations build the bridges your managers can actually staff, the brokering load runs through multiple paths, and the flight-risk connectors are de-loaded and re-engaged. The network is no longer one person deep.`,
                    coach_r4_held:          `<strong>The network held.</strong> A second connector signalled exit and the flow stayed up — because the bridges and apprentices you built carry the load along multiple paths. That's the whole point of network HR: no single departure can sever the firm's knowledge.`,
                    coach_r4_hollow:        `<strong>Right instinct, but the bridges aren't there yet.</strong> You trusted redundant paths that you never actually built — concentration is still high and resilience too low. When the second connector signalled exit, the flow dropped, because the plan was a slide, not a rebuilt network.`,
                    coach_r4_reroute:       `<strong>Re-concentrating onto the last connector is the trap closing on you.</strong> You've made the one remaining central person the entire firm's single point of failure. The next signal — and there's always a next — and the network is gone.`,
                    coach_r4_scramble:      `<strong>Scrambling to re-hire and beg is where the headcount reflex always ends.</strong> The cap hasn't moved, an outsider still arrives with no ties, and begging a flight-risk connector buys a quarter, not a network. You needed the resilient structure built three rounds ago.`,
                    coach_r4_nobridges:     `<strong>You reached for a cross-squad team and the bridges weren't built.</strong> Mobilizing through the network only works if the network exists — and yours is still too thin. The instinct is right; the structure to back it never got funded.`,
                    coach_r4_hero:          `<strong>Putting the last connector on the new client full-time is hero-dependence by another name.</strong> You mobilized through one person again — and made the firm even more fragile to their exit. The bridges were supposed to carry this, not a hero.`,
                    coach_r4_decline:       `<strong>Declining the strategic client is the cost of a network that never got rebuilt.</strong> A firm whose knowledge can't mobilize across squads has to turn away the work that needs it — which is exactly the capability the reorg threatens to freeze.`,
                    coach_r4_case:          `<strong>That's the case the committee can act on.</strong> The four KPIs tell the story — transfer index recovering toward target, density up, concentration down, both connectors secured. You've shown the network holds without a hero, and made the rigid reorganization unnecessary.`,
                    coach_r4_busy:          `<strong>Leading with activity counts the wrong thing.</strong> Rotations run and workshops held are inputs; the committee wants the four outcomes — transfer, density, concentration, retention. Busy-ness isn't resilience, and they can tell the difference.`,
                    coach_r4_caseReorg:     `<strong>Recommending the rigid reorg hands the silos a permanent home.</strong> The entire mandate was to make that unnecessary by rebuilding the network. Conceding it now tells the committee the people-and-ties approach failed — even where your KPIs say otherwise.`,

                    sum_default:            `Intervention made.`,
                    sum_r1_good:            `Read the network, named the concentration risk, targeted the right holes.`,
                    sum_r1_weak:            `Mis-read the network or chased the wrong feature.`,
                    sum_r2_good:            `De-loaded the connectors, funded relational bridges, deployed managers.`,
                    sum_r2_headcount:       `Tried to hire away the problem — the headcount reflex.`,
                    sum_r2_concentration:   `Over-invested in the central few — the concentration trap.`,
                    sum_r2_documentation:   `Bet on a wiki — the documentation fallacy.`,
                    sum_r2_weak:            `Plan under-tuned across the three levers.`,
                    sum_r3_good:            `Apprenticeship, targeted rotations, multipath load — resilient design.`,
                    sum_r3_flood:           `Tried to rotate everyone at once — capacity exhausted.`,
                    sum_r3_concentration:   `Kept the load on heroes — concentration entrenched.`,
                    sum_r3_weak:            `Commitments partially resilient.`,
                    sum_r4_good:            `Network absorbed the shock, mobilized without a hero, made the reorg unnecessary.`,
                    sum_r4_weak:            `Re-concentrated, scrambled, or conceded the reorg.`,

                    insight_network:        `Network rebuilt: you ran the genuine social-network play — read the ties not the org chart, de-loaded the connectors while building relational bridges, transferred tacit knowledge through paired work and rotation, and spread the brokering load across multiple paths. The transfer index recovered, density rose, concentration fell, and the connectors stayed. The rigid reorg is unnecessary.`,
                    insight_concentration:  `Concentration trap: you deepened the network's dependence on a few central people instead of reducing it. Investing everything in the connectors made them more single-point-of-failure than before — and when the network was stress-tested, the over-concentration is exactly what broke. Kaše's warning held: protecting heroes is not the same as building resilience.`,
                    insight_headcount:      `Headcount reflex: you tried to hire the problem away despite the cap, and an outsider arrives with no ties — the network hole stays open for a year while they build relationships. The whole lesson of Network HR is that you fix the network with the people you already have, by managing the ties between them.`,
                    insight_documentation:  `Documentation fallacy: you treated tacit knowledge as if a wiki could capture what made a connector central. The context they carried lives in relationships, not pages — and the transfer index never recovered because the knowledge that mattered never moved. Tacit knowledge transfers through people.`,
                    insight_overreach:      `Bridge-everything overreach: you tried to close too many structural holes at once and exhausted scarce manager and rotation capacity. Half your engineers were in transit instead of delivering, the bridges stayed too thin to carry load, and resilience never built. The right two or three holes beat maximum density.`,
                    insight_mixed:          `A mixed result: some of the network work landed, but the levers — connector investment, bridging mechanism, manager deployment and load redistribution — didn't fully reinforce each other. Enough gaps remained for concentration to persist and for the committee to question whether the network would hold the next departure.`,
                    insight_connectorlost:  `You lost a remaining connector: a second departure on a network still too concentrated severs multiple flows at once and stalls cross-squad work — the same single-point-of-failure that started the crisis. Connector retention and network design are inseparable, not separate workstreams.`,
                    insight_retention:      `You never funded connector retention: a beautiful bridging plan can't help if the central people burn out and leave before the bridges carry load. De-loading and re-engaging the flight-risk connectors was part of the network strategy, not a side issue.`,
                    insight_managers:       `Your line managers stayed inward: deployed only as supervisors, they reinforced their squads' silos instead of brokering across them. A manager is a node with reach into a team — used as a bridge, they move knowledge across squads; used as a supervisor, they entrench the holes.`,
                    insight_shock:          `The stress-test exposed it: when the second connector signalled exit, the reshaped network didn't hold — too much flow still ran through too few people. Resilience is built in the quiet rounds, not improvised when the shock arrives.`,
                    insight_flowfragile:    `Flow recovered but the network stayed fragile: the transfer index is up, yet concentration and thin density mean the next departure could undo it. A high index resting on a few connectors is a number that looks safe and isn't.`,

                    hint_headcount:         `You're reaching for new headcount the CEO won't authorize — and an outsider arrives with no ties. Fix the network with the people you have: de-load the connectors and broker the gaps from inside.`,
                    hint_concentration:     `Connector concentration is dangerously high — you're deepening single-point-of-failure risk. Build redundant paths and de-load the central few before the next exit severs the flow.`,
                    hint_documentation:     `A wiki can't carry tacit knowledge. Move the connectors' context through relationships — paired work, apprenticeship, rotation — or the transfer index won't recover.`,
                    hint_overreach:         `You're trying to bridge too many holes at once and exhausting scarce capacity. Pick the two or three highest-leverage structural holes your managers can actually staff.`,
